  One clarifying question before I do.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether [38;5;153m'...'[39m fits [3minside[0m the budget or hangs off the end. With [38;5;153mmaxLength: 8[39m and input [38;5;153m"hello world"[39m, option
  A returns [38;5;153m"hello..."[39m (8 chars total, 5 of content) and option B returns [38;5;153m"hello wo..."[39m (11 chars). This one matters
  because it's the visible contract — callers sizing a table column or a fixed-width field get a string that overflows
  under B, and changing it later breaks anyone who depended on the old widths.

  - [1mEllipsis counts toward the budget[0m — output never exceeds [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m, which is what a column-width caller needs. Costs
    a little content. Edge case to pin down: [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m smaller than 3.
  - [1mEllipsis appended beyond the budget[0m — you keep a full [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m of real content; output can be [38;5;153mmaxLength + 3[39m.
    Simpler to reason about, no tiny-[38;5;153mmaxLength[39m edge case.

  I recommend the first: [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m reads as a hard ceiling, and a truncation helper that can return something longer
  than the limit is the kind of surprise that shows up as a layout bug rather than a test failure.
